id: decisions/013-record-spine
title: "A fixed spine every record must carry"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'record-shape-uniformity'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: record-shape-uniformity
axis_gloss: "whether every record in the corpus takes the same shape, or each kind of record gets its own"
poles:
  a: per-kind-record-shape
  b: uniform-record-shape
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 23.1
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(22) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  Is there a small set of keys (id, kind, status, proof, boundary) that every
  .yawn record must carry, with kind-specific fields on top, or does each kind
  of record get its own shape?

why_it_matters: >
  templates/ ships four incompatible shapes with nothing ranking them, and the
  advertised first template omits id, kind and status even though 182, 180 and
  176 of 194 files carry them. Once answered, a bot that validates or ranks
  records knows which keys it may rely on across the corpus, and a child Yawn
  inherits a spine it can extend rather than a shape it must guess from
  siblings.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    17 judgments sit at per-kind-record-shape (1 authored) and 5 at
    uniform-record-shape (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether boundary belongs in the spine (the micro examples keep it, the
    objective-holon example replaces it with guardrails), and whether the two JSON
    templates are a second grammar or just another kind.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Each kind of record defines its own shape. templates/ holds one template
      per kind; no top-level key is required of every record, and the questions:,
      boundary: and proof: blocks appear only where the kind needs them.
    cost: "templates/node.yawn must list the kinds and their shapes; the 66 files without proof: and the templates without id/kind/status stay as they are."
    risk: "No corpus-wide checker is possible; a bot must know every kind before it can read any record."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Per-kind validators only (the model validate-mental-models.mjs already follows); nothing corpus-wide."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0194"
      - "J0331"
      - "J0348"
      - "J0405"
      - "J0447"
      - "J0527"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Every .yawn record takes one shape: the same top-level keys in the same
      order, as the micro examples (move, boundary, proof, replay) and the two
      mental models (eight fixed questions) already do. Kind-specific content lives
      under those keys, not beside them.
    cost: "Rewrite every template into one shape; the JSON templates and the q-space templates cannot conform without changing grammar."
    risk: "Records that need a different shape (an intake form, a receipt with hashes) get forced into keys that do not fit, and the uniformity becomes cosmetic."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that every active .yawn has exactly the fixed key set at top level."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0300"
      - "J0319"
      - "J0414"
      - "J0801"
      - "J0932"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Every active .yawn record carries a spine: id, kind, status, updated,
      proof, and boundary. Each kind may add its own keys beyond the spine.
      templates/node.yawn names the spine and says which of the four template shapes
      is canonical for which kind. Files that cannot carry the spine (the two JSON
      templates) are listed by name.
    cost: "Add proof: to 66 files or list them; add id/kind/status to templates/basic.yawn and the other spine-less templates; write the ranking into templates/node.yawn."
    risk: "The spine is added as empty placeholders that satisfy the test and say nothing."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ (tests/record-spine.test.mjs) that every active .yawn outside a named exclusion list carries the spine keys, modelled on scripts/validate-mental-models.mjs."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0300"
      - "J0319"
      - "J0414"
      - "J0801"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Per-kind shape is the actual practice (17 revealed judgments) and uniform
    shape is enforced in exactly one place (scripts/validate-mental-models.mjs,
    proof-attached J0319). Neither pole is authored beyond one judgment each. But
    GROUND_TRUTH FACT 13 shows the corpus already has a de facto spine: id in
    182/194, kind in 180, status in 176, proof in 128. C writes that spine down
    and leaves per-kind extension free, which is what the outcome-attached
    judgments (the declaration written in protocol shape, the five-section commit
    body) show the repo does when it means it. B would break the JSON and q-space
    templates; A gives up the keys that already exist.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "templates/node.yawn names the spine keys and states which template shape is canonical for which kind."
    - "A test in tests/ fails on an active .yawn missing a spine key unless the file is on a named exclusion list, and passes."
    - "The 66 files without proof: either carry one or are on that list by name."
    - "templates/basic.yawn carries id, kind and status."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new template lands with a fifth top-level shape and nothing in tests/
    notices.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0194"
  - "J0331"
  - "J0348"
  - "J0405"
  - "J0447"
  - "J0527"
  - "J0300"
  - "J0319"
  - "J0414"
  - "J0801"
  - "J0932"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
